-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Overfitting:** Adding more splats beyond the optimal budget causes the model to memorize noise rather than signal, indicated by a growing gap between training PSNR and held-out PSNR [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Noise Assumption:** Blind-spot cross-validation assumes noise is independent across voxels [S1, unofficial]. On fused or deconvolved volumes, a held-out peak is not guaranteed [S1, unofficial]. If a noise floor is estimable, it caps the budget from above to avoid reproducing noise [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Resolution Limits:** Structures thinner than the local Gaussian scale (e.g., individual microtubules) blur [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Convergence:** Convergence is rapid; at 2,000 iterations, training PSNR is within 0.5 dB of the 20,000-iteration value for most volume-capacity cells [S1, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
+
+### Volumetric Gaussian Splatting (AVIS)
+Focuses on volumetric data representation using Gaussians, differing in its specific handling of volume density and opacity compared to the scalar amplitude approach in microscopy. [Link](1875-volumetric-gaussian-splatting-avis.md)
+
+### UltraGauss Rendering
+Optimizes rendering speed for large-scale Gaussian scenes, potentially differing in the culling and rasterization strategies used for microscopy volumes. [Link](5170-ultragauss-rendering.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-
-### Time and Memory Complexity
-*   **Fitting Time:** Median 2.4 minutes per volume at a 32K splat budget on an NVIDIA RTX 3070 (8 GiB) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Adding capacity (splats) outperforms adding iterations. 16K splats × 500 iterations beat 4K splats × 20,000 iterations by an average of +3.03 dB across 17 volumes at 10× fewer splat-iterations [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Fits store volumes 6–340× smaller (median 99.3×) at 26–67 dB PSNR [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Measured Performance
-*   **Tribolium embryo nuclei:** 51.4 dB PSNR at 32K splats, 54.9 dB at 512K, 56.0 dB at 2M splats [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Kidney nuclei:** 34.8 dB PSNR at 32K splats, 45.2 dB at 512K splats [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rendering:** On an RTX 3070 at 720p HDR, Gaussian splats maintain 60 FPS (16.7 ms budget) through $10^6$ elements [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Strengths and Limitations
-*   **Strengths:** Direct GPU rasterization without voxel grids; suitable for streaming where bandwidth and memory are constrained; fast fitting; high compression ratios [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Structures thinner than the local Gaussian scale blur; not suitable for precise quantification or measurement; fitting is asymmetric (expensive fitting, cheap viewing) [S1, unofficial].
-
-### When to Use
-*   Use for interactive web visualization and sharing of large n-dimensional microscopy datasets [S1, unofficial].
-*   Use when bandwidth and client-side memory are constrained [S1, unofficial].
-*   Do not use for precise quantitative analysis, segmentation, or tracking; use source voxels for those tasks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Fitting is the expensive side. Convergence is rapid; at 2,000 iterations, training PSNR is within 0.5 dB of the 20,000-iteration value for 77 of 85 volume-capacity cells [S1, unofficial]. Adding capacity (more splats) outperforms adding iterations: 16K splats × 500 iterations beat 4K splats × 20,000 iterations by +3.03 dB average across 17 volumes at 10× fewer splat-iterations [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity**: The representation is compact. Median compression ratio is 99.3× (range 6–340×) across 17 volumes from 12 datasets [S1, unofficial]. For example, a Kidney nuclei volume (199 MB raw) is compressed to 18.4 MB (11× smaller) with 2.04M splats achieving 56.0 dB PSNR [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance**: On an NVIDIA RTX 3070 (8 GiB), median fitting time is 2.4 minutes per volume at a 32K budget [S1, unofficial]. Rendering maintains 60 FPS (16.7 ms budget) through $10^6$ elements at 720p HDR [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Direct GPU rasterization without voxel grids; suitable for streaming and interactive web visualization; significant compression; fast convergence.
+*   **Limitations**: Structures thinner than the local Gaussian scale (e.g., individual microtubules) blur; the representation is for looking, not measuring; fitting is asymmetric (expensive fitting, cheap viewing); blind-spot cross-validation may not find a clear peak on fused/deconvolved volumes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use**: Interactive web visualization of large nD microscopy volumes; sharing data via static file hosting; compression for storage.
+*   **When not to use**: Quantification, segmentation, or tracking tasks where precise voxel-level accuracy is required; quick inspection of a single stack where fitting time is prohibitive [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Splatting for Microscopy is part of the Luxar framework, an open-source Python system designed for scalable interactive web visualization of multidimensional scientific data [S1, unofficial]. It adapts nD Gaussian splatting for microscopy, validating the approach on 17 volumes from 12 datasets spanning spinning-disk, confocal, light-sheet, and structured-illumination modalities [S1, unofficial]. The method uses blind-spot cross-validation to determine the optimal splat count, balancing PSNR and overfitting [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced in the Luxar framework, an open-source Python system for compiling multidimensional scientific data into compact archives for web visualization [S1, unofficial]. It adapts nD Gaussian splatting for microscopy, validating the approach on 17 volumes from 12 datasets spanning spinning-disk, confocal, light-sheet, and structured-illumination modalities [S1, unofficial]. The approach builds on the principle that fluorescence volumes can be represented as mixtures of anisotropic Gaussians, with the number of splats determined by blind-spot cross-validation to separate signal from noise [S1, unofficial]. It complements desktop tools like napari and Fiji, and voxel-pyramid web viewers like Neuroglancer and Viv [S1, unofficial].
 
